{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Enrique Malek International Airport (IATA: DAV, ICAO: MPDA) ("Spanish: Aeropuerto Internacional Enrique Malek") is an international airport located in the city of David, Chiriquí in the Republic of Panama.","title":"Enrique Malek International Airport"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The former terminal was demolished for a new one to be built.","title":"Enrique Malek International Airport"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" When construction had finished, daily flights from Air Panama started arriving.","title":"Enrique Malek International Airport"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Now the international flight terminal receives numerous daily flights from Copa Airlines and Air Panama.","title":"Enrique Malek International Airport"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Also car rentals commenced operations in the new terminal.","title":"Enrique Malek International Airport"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Australian Two Airlines Policy (or Two Airlines Agreement) was a policy of Australian Federal Governments from the late 1940s to the 1990s.","title":"Two Airlines Policy"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Under the policy, only two airlines were allowed to operate flights between state capital city and major regional city airports. For most of the period of the policy, the "two airlines" were the privately owned Ansett Airlines and the government-owned Trans Australia Airlines.","title":"Two Airlines Policy"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Though persisting for some decades, the policy finally fell into abeyance in the world-wide airline deregulations of the 1990s.","title":"Two Airlines Policy"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Air Panama is a regional airline based at Albrook "Marcos A. Gelabert" International Airport in Panama, and is currently the second-largest air carrier in the country, surpassed only by Copa Airlines.","title":"Air Panama"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The carrier offers both scheduled and charter passenger flights to more than 31 destinations from its hub at Albrook International Airport.","title":"Air Panama"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Aeroperlas (acronym for Aerolíneas Islas de Las Perlas) was a regional airline based in Panama City, Panama.","title":"Aeroperlas"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was the third largest airline of the country, only surpassed by Air Panama and Copa Airlines.","title":"Aeroperlas"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" From its hub at Marcos A. Gelabert and Enrique Malek International airports, Aeroperlas operated over 50 daily scheduled flights to 15 domestic destinations, as well as charter and courier flights.","title":"Aeroperlas"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It operated services as part of the Grupo TACA Regional Airlines system.","title":"Aeroperlas"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Puerto Rico Air Management Services (PRAMS) is an Air Charter and cargo operator out of the Miami International Airport.","title":"Prams Air"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The airline was founded in 2005.","title":"Prams Air"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Its operating certificate allows it to fly the USA, the entire Caribbean (including Cuba) and South America.","title":"Prams Air"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The company also has a unique operating permit, that allows it to operate flights from the United States mainland to Cuba.","title":"Prams Air"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Operating charter flights that usually carry excess baggage and other cargo for companies that arrange these unique flights leaving the Miami International Airport to the otherwise U.S. embargoed Caribbean island.","title":"Prams Air"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The majority of Chinese nationals currently residing in Samoa are businessmen, labour workers and shopowners in the south western island nation of Samoa, and there are at least 30,000 people in Samoa who are of mixed Samoan and Chinese descent, although they are classified as ethnic Samoans in official census.","title":"Chinese in Samoa"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Around the world, about 25% of all Samoans claim Chinese ancestry.","title":"Chinese in Samoa"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Nearly all Chinese nationals in Samoa reside within the Apia municipal area; neighbouring American Samoa, also has a small population of Chinese expatriates.","title":"Chinese in Samoa"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Samoas Legislative Capital city of Apia signed a treaty on 31:08:2015 with a delegation from Shenzhen, China making the Legislative capital of Apia and The city of Shenzhen, Guangdong, China sister cities.","title":"Chinese in Samoa"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The treaty will help bring Chinese tourists to boast Samoas growing Tourism industry and will also bring economic growth to Samoa also bringing stronger ties between the two cities.","title":"Chinese in Samoa"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Shenzhen Airlines is also set to operate flights from Shenzhen International airport and Faleolo International Airport, Apia.","title":"Chinese in Samoa"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" The Chinese community in Samoa is growing and becoming economically strong.","title":"Chinese in Samoa"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" A new wave of Chinese migrants moving to Samoa are coming from the northern region, bringing their culture and languages with them.","title":"Chinese in Samoa"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" There are no Chinese schools in Samoa but an estimated 98.7% of Chinese expatriates and migrants send their children and youth to Robert Louis Stevenson School, Samoa which is a private school with an Australasian and Samoan curriculum, the tuition fee is WST700-845.","title":"Chinese in Samoa"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" There is a primary campus located in the village of Lotopa, Faleata District and the secondary campus in the urban village of Tafaigata.","title":"Chinese in Samoa"},{"sentence_id":10,"text":" Notable Chinese businesses include Frankie's Supermarket and Wholesale, Alan wholesale and Treasure Garden company as well as other small businesses and restaurants.","title":"Chinese in Samoa"}