{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"This article lists the members of President Donald Trump's Cabinet.","title":"Cabinet of Donald Trump"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Trump assumed office on January 20, 2017.","title":"Cabinet of Donald Trump"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Shadow Cabinet of Thailand (Thai: คณะรัฐมนตรีเงา or รัฐบาลเงา) is an unofficial group of senior opposition party's spokespeople who form an alternative cabinet to the government.","title":"Shadow Cabinet of Thailand"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The shadow cabinet members shadow or mark each individual member of the government.","title":"Shadow Cabinet of Thailand"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" After the People's Power Party's win in the 2007 general election, the Democrat Party became the sole official opposition party.","title":"Shadow Cabinet of Thailand"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Abhisit Vejjajiva, leader of the Democrat Party, expressed his intention to set up a shadow cabinet to track the new administration's performance, to propose better solutions, and to provide the Democrat's standpoint on each government decision.","title":"Shadow Cabinet of Thailand"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The shadow cabinet line-up was announced on February 8, 2008 after the Samak's administration had officially assumed office.","title":"Shadow Cabinet of Thailand"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" On the 15 December 2008, Abhisit was elected by the House of Representatives to become the next Prime Minister, within a week a Cabinet will be formed which will include many members his Shadow Cabinet.","title":"Shadow Cabinet of Thailand"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" However a day later the For Thais Party (the opposition party) MP Jatuporn Promphan stated that his party will not be forming a Shadow Cabinet.","title":"Shadow Cabinet of Thailand"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Stone v. Trump (1:17-cv-02459-MJG) is a lawsuit filed on August 28, 2017 in the United States District Court for the District of Maryland.","title":"Stone v. Trump"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The lawsuit alleges that President Donald Trump's ban on transgender personnel joining the U.S. military violates their equal protection and due process rights.","title":"Stone v. Trump"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The American Civil Liberties Union (ACLU) of Maryland filed the suit on behalf of Petty Officer First Class Brock Stone, an 11-year veteran of the U.S. Navy, and several other transgender service members.","title":"Stone v. Trump"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" In addition to President Trump, the suit names as defendants the Secretaries of Defense (James Mattis), the Army (Ryan McCarthy, acting), the Navy (Richard Spencer), and the Air Force (Heather Wilson).","title":"Stone v. Trump"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Ralph Mattis (August 24, 1890 – September 13, 1960), known also as Matty Mattis, was a professional baseball outfielder whose career spanned seven seasons, one of which was spent in Major League Baseball (MLB) with the Pittsburgh Rebels (1914).","title":"Ralph Mattis"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" In his only season in the majors, Mattis batted .247 with 14 runs scored, 21 hits, four doubles, one triple, and eight runs batted in (RBIs) in 36 games played.","title":"Ralph Mattis"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The majority of his career was played in the minor leagues.","title":"Ralph Mattis"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" He played with the Richmond Colts (1911), Newport News Shipbuilders (1912), Roanoke Tigers (1913), Parksley Spuds (1923–24), and Crisfield Crabbers (1925) over his career in the minors.","title":"Ralph Mattis"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Combined between those teams, Mattis batted .303 with 698 hits in 610 games played.","title":"Ralph Mattis"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" During his career, he stood at 5 ft and weighed 172 lb .","title":"Ralph Mattis"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" He batted and threw right-handed.","title":"Ralph Mattis"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" Mattis served as a manager for one season with the Parksley Spuds (1923).","title":"Ralph Mattis"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Duke Records was an American record label, started in Memphis, Tennessee, in 1952 by David James Mattis (WDIA program director and DJ) and Bill Fitzgerald, owners of Tri-State Recording Company.","title":"Duke Records"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Their first release was Roscoe Gordon singing "Hey Fat Girl", issued on Duke R-1, later amended to R-101.","title":"Duke Records"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"James Norman "Jim" Mattis (born September 8, 1950) is a retired United States Marine Corps general who is the 26th and current United States Secretary of Defense, serving in the Cabinet of Donald Trump.","title":"James Mattis"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Mattis was previously the 11th Commander of United States Central Command during the presidency of Barack Obama, and was responsible for American military operations in the Middle East, Northeast Africa, and Central Asia.","title":"James Mattis"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: January 20, 2017
