{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"James Alexander MacLaren (4 January 1870 – 8 July 1952) was an English cricketer who played first-class cricket for Lancashire County Cricket Club between 1891 and 1894.","title":"James MacLaren (cricketer)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Born in Whalley Range, Manchester, and the eldest of seven brothers, MacLaren was a keen cricketer from his youngest days.","title":"James MacLaren (cricketer)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" He was particularly close to his younger brother Archie, who later captained England.","title":"James MacLaren (cricketer)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" He attended Elstree School and Harrow School, where he coached as a cricketer and was later joined by Archie.","title":"James MacLaren (cricketer)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" He also received coaching at Old Trafford Cricket Ground, where his father was treasurer of Lancashire County Cricket Club.","title":"James MacLaren (cricketer)"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" He played in the Harrow first team between 1886 and 1888, and was appointed captain in his final year.","title":"James MacLaren (cricketer)"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Upon leaving Harrow, he played on four occasions for Lancashire between 1891 and 1894 but had little success.","title":"James MacLaren (cricketer)"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" On one occasion in 1894, he opened the batting with his brother Archie, who was captain of Lancashire by that stage.","title":"James MacLaren (cricketer)"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" In 1900, he qualified as a doctor of medicine.","title":"James MacLaren (cricketer)"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" He died at the age of 82 following a fall.","title":"James MacLaren (cricketer)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"William Harold "Bill" Ponsford (19 October 1900 – 6 April 1991) was an Australian cricketer.","title":"Bill Ponsford"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Usually playing as an opening batsman, he formed a successful and long-lived partnership opening the batting for Victoria and Australia with Bill Woodfull, his friend and state and national captain.","title":"Bill Ponsford"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Ponsford is the only player to twice break the world record for the highest individual score in first-class cricket; Ponsford and Brian Lara are the only cricketers to twice score 400 runs in an innings.","title":"Bill Ponsford"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Ponsford holds the Australian record for a partnership in Test cricket, set in 1934 in combination with Donald Bradman(451 for 2nd wicket)—the man who broke many of Ponsford's other individual records.In fact,he along with Don Bradman set the record for the highest partnership ever for any wicket in Test cricket history when playing in away soil (451 runs for the second wicket)","title":"Bill Ponsford"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"John "Jack" Henry Webb Fingleton OBE (28 April 190822 November 1981) was an Australian cricketer who was trained as a journalist and became a political and cricket commentator after the end of his playing career.","title":"Jack Fingleton"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" A stubborn opening batsman known for his dour defensive approach, he scored five Test centuries, representing Australia in 18 Tests between 1932 and 1938.","title":"Jack Fingleton"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" He was also known for his involvement in several cricket diplomacy incidents in his career, accused of leaking the infamous verbal exchange between Australian captain Bill Woodfull and English manager Plum Warner during the acrimonious Bodyline series, and later of causing sectarian tension within the team by leading a group of players of Irish Catholic descent in undermining the leadership of the Protestant Don Bradman.","title":"Jack Fingleton"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" In retirement, Fingleton became a prominent political commentator in Canberra, with links to Australian prime ministers.","title":"Jack Fingleton"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The author of many cricket books, he is regarded as one of Australia's finest cricket writers, with a perceptive and occasionally sardonic style, marked by persistent criticisms of Bradman.","title":"Jack Fingleton"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Murray Hedgcock (born 23 February 1931) is an Australian cricket writer and journalist.","title":"Murray Hedgcock"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He was born in south Melbourne and grew up in various country towns in Victoria.","title":"Murray Hedgcock"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The test cricketer Bill Woodfull was the headmaster of one of his schools.","title":"Murray Hedgcock"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" After leaving school, he worked briefly in a bank before becoming a journalist.","title":"Murray Hedgcock"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" From 1966 until his retirement in 1991, he was posted to London.","title":"Murray Hedgcock"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" He wrote regularly for "The Australian", Wisden and "The Cricketer".","title":"Murray Hedgcock"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"William Maldon "Bill" Woodfull OBE (22 August 1897 – 11 August 1965) was an Australian cricketer of the 1920s and 1930s.","title":"Bill Woodfull"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He captained both Victoria and Australia, and was best known for his dignified and moral conduct during the tumultuous bodyline series in 1932–33 that almost saw the end of Anglo-Australian cricketing ties.","title":"Bill Woodfull"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Trained as a schoolteacher, Woodfull was known for his benevolent attitude towards his players, and his patience and defensive technique as an opening batsman.","title":"Bill Woodfull"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Woodfull was not a flamboyant player, but was known for his calm, unruffled style and his reliability in difficult situations.","title":"Bill Woodfull"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" His opening pairing with fellow Victorian Bill Ponsford for both his state and Australia remains one of the most successful in history.","title":"Bill Woodfull"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" While not known for his tactical skills, Woodfull was widely admired by his players and observers for his sportsmanship and ability to mould a successful and loyal team through the strength of his character.","title":"Bill Woodfull"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Australia won the 1930 Ashes series against England, winning two of the matches and losing one, with the other two tests drawn.","title":"Australian cricket team in England in 1930"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The Australian tourists were captained by Bill Woodfull, while the home side were led by Percy Chapman, who was dropped in favour of Bob Wyatt in the final Test.","title":"Australian cricket team in England in 1930"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Bill Ponsford
